
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last clarifying question before I work up approaches.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what happens to the user's login when the tracking POST fails. Network calls fail, and this one is on the
  critical path of your auth flow. This decides whether [38;5;153mlogin[39m becomes coupled to tracking's reliability.

  - [1mFire-and-forget[0m — dispatch the event, never await it, swallow errors. Buys a login flow whose reliability is
    unchanged by tracking. Costs silently lost events: you'll never know what you didn't record, so the data has gaps
    you can't detect.
  - [1mAwait and surface, but don't block[0m — await delivery, log failures to the console, let login succeed regardless. Buys
    visibility into loss. Costs a small delay on the login path.
  - [1mTracking failure fails the login[0m — no record, no entry. Buys a guarantee that every successful login is recorded.
    Costs: your auth becomes unavailable whenever the tracking service is down. Only justifiable under a hard compliance
    rule.

  [1mMy recommendation: fire-and-forget, with failures logged.[0m Effectively option one plus option two's visibility — the
  event dispatch doesn't gate the UI, but errors aren't swallowed silently. Tracking should never be able to lock users
  out; the third option trades a real capability for a bookkeeping guarantee.

  While you consider that, I'm checking whether I can get an independent second opinion on the architecture from Codex
  before I write up approaches.
